# Netlify custom response headers. Rules match the REQUEST path, so they apply
# to SPA routes even though every path rewrites to /index.html via _redirects.
#
# Why noindex on the card and live-page routes: those pages publish a
# customer's phone number and email address. Keeping them out of the search
# index is a privacy decision, not an SEO one, and it holds regardless of what
# AdSense decides about this domain.
#
# Why a header and not just robots.txt: robots.txt blocks the CRAWL, which
# means Google never fetches the page and therefore never reads a noindex.
# A blocked URL can still be listed as a bare link with no snippet if Google
# finds it referenced somewhere. X-Robots-Tag is the directive that actually
# removes a page from the index, and it only works if crawling is ALLOWED.
#
# Sequencing, do not skip: the Disallow lines in robots.txt must stay until
# this header is confirmed live (see the verify command in robots.txt).
# Dropping the Disallow first would open these pages to indexing with nothing
# in place to stop it.

/c/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

/c1/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

/p/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
